298†Contents L471:   * cite299†Preface L472:   * cite300†Introduction L473:   * cite301†1 The language of symmetry L474:   * cite302†2 A delightful fiction L475:   * cite303†3 Double spirals and Möbius maps L476:   * cite304†4 The Schottky dance pages 96 to 107 L477:   * cite305†4 The Schottky dance pages 107 to 120 L478:   * cite306†5 Fractal dust and infinite words L479:   * cite307†6 Indra's necklace L480:   * cite308†7 The glowing gasket L481:   * cite309†8 Playing with parameters pages 224 to 244 L482:   * cite310†8 Playing with parameters pages 245 to 267 L483:   * cite311†9 Accidents will happen pages 268 to 291 L484:   * cite312†9 Accidents will happen pages 291 to 296 L485:   * cite313†9 Accidents will happen pages 296 to 309 L486:   * cite314†10 Between the cracks pages 310 to 320 L487:   * cite315†10 Between the cracks pages 320 to 330 L488:   * cite316†10 Between the cracks pages 331 to 340 L489:   * cite317†10 Between the cracks pages 340 to 345 L490:   * cite318†10 Between the cracks pages 345 to 352 L491:   * cite319†11 Crossing boundaries pages 353 to 365 L492:   * cite320†11 Crossing boundaries 365 to 372 L493:   * cite321†12 Epilogue L494:   * cite322†Index L495:   * cite323†Road map L496: cite324†Get access L497: \nL498: * * *\nL499: ## Summary\nL500: \nL501: What kind of a book is this?\nL502: This is a book about serious mathematics, but one which we hope will be enjoyed by as wide an audience as possible. It is the story of our computer aided explorations of a family of unusually symmetrical shapes, which arise when two spiral motions of a very special kind are allowed to interact. These shapes display intricate ‘fractal’ complexity on every scale from very large to very small. Their visualisation forms part of a century-old dream conceived by the great German geometer Felix Klein.\nL503: Sometimes the interaction of the two spiral motions is quite regular and harmonious, sometimes it is total disorder and sometimes – and this is the most intriguing case – it has layer upon layer of structure teetering on the very brink of chaos.\nL504: As we progressed in our explorations, the pictures that our computer programs produced were so striking that we wanted to tell our tale in a manner which could be appreciated beyond the narrow confines of a small circle of specialists. You can get a foretaste of their variety by taking a look at the Road Map on the final page.\nL505: Mathematicians often use the word ‘beautiful’ in talking about their proofs and ideas, but in this case our judgment has been confirmed by a number of unbiassed and definitely non-mathematical people. The visual beauty of the pictures is a veneer which covers a core of important and elegant mathematical ideas; it has been our aspiration to convey some of this inner aesthetics as well.\nL506: * * *\nL507: ## Information\nL508: \nL509: Type\nL510:     Chapter\nL511: \nL512: Information\nL513: \nL514: \nL515: cite325†Indra's Pearls L516: \nL517: The Vision of Felix Klein\nL518: \nL519: , pp. vii - xiv\nL520: \nL521: DOI: cite326†https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107050051.001 [Opens in a new window]†doi.org L522: \nL523: Publisher: Cambridge University Press\nL524: \nL525: Print publication year: 2002\nL526: \nL527: * * *\nL528: \nL529: ## Accessibility standard: Unknown\nL530: ### Why this information is here\nL531: \nL532: This section outlines the accessibility features of this content - including support for screen readers, full keyboard navigation and high-contrast display options. This may not be relevant for you.\nL533: \nL534: ### Accessibility Information\nL535: \nL536: Accessibility compliance for the PDF of this chapter is currently unknown and may be updated in the future.\nL537: \nL538: # Cited by\nL539: \nL540: Loading...\nL541: # Save book to Kindle\nL542: \nL543: To save this book to your Kindle, first ensure no-reply@cambridge.org is added to your A